Workflow 1: Order Lifecycle Management

Order Lifecycle Stages:

•	Order Received
◦	Payment Status [Paid/COD]
◦	BLACKLISTED NUMBERS VALIDATION
•	Fulfillment [Fulfilled/Unfulfilled]
◦	Fragile/Special Handling Gate
•	LABEL GENERATION FOR PRINTING
•	Ready for Pickup
•	Pickup In Progress
•	Picked Up
•	Shipment Dispatched
•	In Transit
•	Out For Delivery
•	Delivery Attempt
•	Delivered/Failed -> Returned

The first workflow we will be working on in our project will be the backbone of a courier delivery management system is the order lifecycle management, through these stages we will be displaying the current
status of the order in our application. The order lifecycle begins at Order Received in the system. At this stage, all essential details such as customer name, phone number, address, items, and payment type
are recorded. The order is then marked under Payment Status [Paid/COD].  Immediately after order entry, the system performs a BLACKLISTED NUMBERS VALIDATION to avoid catering orders of defaulters, by matching 
the customer’s phone number against a stored list of blacklisted or defaulter numbers. If the number is flagged, the order is visibly labeled as Blacklisted within the application, if a blacklisted order is
clicked on for being fulfilled, a pop-up alert is given on the screen before proceeding further. 

If the order clears the validation step, the order moves into the Fulfillment Stage, where it is marked either FULFILLED or UNFULFILLED depending on stock availability.  If fulfilled, the system checks whether
the shipment requires special care through a Fragile / Special Handling. This stage ensures that fragile or sensitive items are clearly identified before shipment preparation. These flags will be of consideration
for influence in both packaging instructions and rider assignment.

We will be working in depth on a crucial step in the delivery process, LABEL GENERATION FOR PRINTING, which acts as a mandatory checkpoint before pickup,  the system uses a fixed label design template. The 
template layout remains constant, while order-specific data such as sender, receiver, phone number, city, COD amount, product nature, tracking number are dynamically injected into the template through backend
code. Markers like fragile/handle with care may also be reflected in the generated labels. When the dispatcher clicks “Generate Label,” the system automatically populates the template fields with the respective
order details and produces a printable PDF/image file.

After label printing, the order status changes to Ready for Pickup. The dispatcher manually toggles the order through real-world states: Pickup In Progress, Picked Up, and Shipment Dispatched. Once dispatched, 
the shipment moves into In Transit, followed by Out for Delivery and Delivery Attempt. These states reflect the real-time condition of the order and are updated manually within the application to mirror real-life
progress. The final stage in the forward flow is Delivered or Failed/Returned. If successfully delivered, the order lifecycle closes then and there. If delivery fails, the order transitions automatically direct 
into the returns management workflow described ahead.

Workflow 2: Rider Assignment Workflow

The second workflow we will be working on in our project is the core of a courier delivery management system which is Rider assignment for orders. It begins after the order reaches a state such as “Ready for Pickup.” Once the order is mapped, the system organizes riders based on fixed locations or depots, meaning each rider belongs to a defined geographical zone. Orders are filtered by zone to ensure assignments are location-appropriate. This implementation plan for this assignment is an intermediate approach involving a Zone-based Rider Pool used with Scored Recommendations. The user will manually confirm one of the riders displayed according to the ranked results, these ranked results may be subject to rider’s availability and capacity. At a glance the key steps in determining the rider, are:
	•	Rider Locations & Delivery Address Mapping
	•	Scored Recommendations based on a Distance Metric between Rider’s Location & Delivery Address
	•	Manual Confirmation
For each eligible order, the system calculates the distance between the order location and each rider in the zone using a distance metric. Riders are ranked based on proximity, with the closest rider receiving the highest recommendation score. In the interface, when a dispatcher clicks an order, a side panel displays a specific number for example, top three recommended riders, along with metrics such as:
	•	Distance (e.g., 24.2 km)
	•	Current Load (e.g., 3/28 orders)
	•	Capacity Usage (e.g., 18kg / 40kg)
This allows informed and semi-intelligent assignment decisions.
Each rider has predefined capacity limits reflecting truck size and maximum order count. If a rider has reached their limit, delivering another order, or is unavailable (e.g., shift ended), the system automatically considers the next ranked rider in the distance list. This ensures load balancing and manages assignment equally amongst the riders. Although recommendations are automated, final assignment is done through manual confirmation by the dispatcher. This structure tries to closely replicate a real life scenario of courier operations with incorporating a ranking logic.

